$NetBSD$

Experimental GPL XFS for NetBSD PUFFS
====================================

This starts a direct port of SGI/Linux XFS, using Linux v6.12 as a pinned
baseline. See README.import for source provenance and COPYING for the license.
The old http://oss.sgi.com/projects/xfs/ URL is historical; the maintained
implementation is in Linux's fs/xfs tree.

The target is a user-space filesystem using NetBSD PUFFS/libpuffs. The GPL
XFS reader and future recovery code run in a mount_xfs server, with the
existing PUFFS kernel interface handling filesystem requests. The source
has moved from sys/external/gpl2/xfs to external/gpl2/xfs for this purpose.
The current milestone adds an experimental read-only mount_xfs server around
the portable image reader. It is built explicitly, outside the default build.

Current milestone
-----------------

puffs/mount_xfs.c serves regular files, directories and symlinks from a
quiescent regular image through libpuffs. It requires a clean journal before
mounting, opens the image read-only, and forces read-only/nodev/nosuid/noatime.
There is no journal bypass or write path. Verified inline user attributes
and the root directory's trusted xfs:autofsck property are supported. The
property accepts none, check, optimize and repair, and is exposed as metadata
without triggering any maintenance. Inodes with ACLs, security labels,
unknown trusted attributes, parent pointers, or nonempty external attribute
forks are rejected. Do not remove security attributes from user images to
make them mountable.

The adapter implements lookup/getattr/access/open/read/readdir/readlink,
statvfs, sync, unmount and node reclamation. It preserves ownership, mode,
link count, allocated bytes, generation and timestamps, including bigtime
and v3 birth times. Legacy inodes do not acquire invented birth times.
Search/access checks use the supplied PUFFS credentials. Hard links reuse
one puffs_node per inode, and reads preserve access through existing file
descriptors after a credential change. Kernel namei handles paths/symlinks.
Directory offsets and cookies are entry ordinals on the immutable image;
resumed reads scan the directory and verify it before publishing the result.
All mutations return EROFS.

getextattr/listextattr expose raw XFS names through NetBSD's native extattr
API: user attributes use EXTATTR_NAMESPACE_USER, and xfs:autofsck uses
EXTATTR_NAMESPACE_SYSTEM. User attributes require file read permission;
system attributes require privileged credentials. Checks apply to descriptor
operations too, including after a credential change. Size queries, empty and
binary values, truncated reads and both list delimiter formats are supported.
Names are not prefixed or aliased: use "fixture" in the user namespace and
"xfs:autofsck" in the system namespace. Linux user./trusted. name-prefix
translation is not implemented. Missing names on accepted inodes return ENODATA.

Callbacks are serialized, use synchronous pread, and never yield while using
shared scratch. Name/page caches are disabled and attribute TTLs remain zero.
Memory mapping and execution from the mount are unsupported. An advisory
shared image lock is supplemental: callers must keep the image quiescent.
Detected size/mtime/ctime changes permanently fail service with ESTALE;
this is not snapshot isolation. SIGINT/SIGTERM request forced unmounts.
NetBSD currently overrides statvfs's name limit with libpuffs' initial
MAXNAMLEN (511), despite the callback reporting XFS's actual 255-byte limit.

The upstream superblock decoder, version checks and quota conversion run
through a small NetBSD compatibility header. A read-only probe adds bounded
input handling, v5 CRC32C verification over the complete sector, basic
geometry validation and data-device bounds checks. It handles unaligned
input, checks disk-controlled shifts, and preserves the caller's output on
failure. Filesystem block numbers are decoded as AG/block pairs, including
when the allocation-group size is not a power of two.

A bounded read callback now supplies data-device I/O. xfs_reader_open loads
and validates the primary superblock; xfs_read_blocks checks encoded addresses
and rejects reads crossing an AG boundary, the filesystem end, or the device
end. Short reads and callback failures propagate as errors. The caller owns
the read-only device and a sector-sized scratch buffer; there are no large
stack buffers or hidden allocations.

xfs_read_ag reads and verifies AGF, AGI and AGFL sectors, checking CRC32C,
metadata UUID, sequence number, actual AG length, free-list ring, tree roots
and depth limits, counters, and unlinked inode pointer bounds. Optional
finobt/rmapbt/reflink/inobtcount fields follow the superblock feature bits.
Unused v4 free-list slots are ignored; active slots are always checked. The
verified output is published only after all three reads and verifiers succeed.

xfs_read_inode verifies allocation through the inode btree, including sparse
chunks and the free-inode bitmap, before reading the inode. It checks inode
identity, v3 CRC/UUID, fork geometry, extent counts, feature flags and extent
hints. Inline extents are validated immediately; bmap roots are checked at
inode load and external bmap blocks are verified as file reads traverse them.
Both tree readers check CRCs, owners, physical addresses, UUIDs, sibling
bounds, record counts, key order and parent/child ranges. Decreasing tree
levels bound traversal even if corrupted pointers form a cycle.

xfs_read_attrs verifies an inode's complete shortform attribute fork before
emitting any entries: total size, count, entry bounds, namespace flags, NUL
names and duplicate names. Values remain opaque bytes. An empty retained
extent fork is accepted; external attribute blocks and parent pointers are
unsupported. No additional I/O or allocation is needed after xfs_read_inode.
xfs_attrs_check_readonly applies the separate mount policy to every attribute
before the adapter publishes an inode. An allowed property cannot conceal an
unsupported ACL or a malformed later entry. The raw inode/path inspector
does not call this policy and is still not an access-control boundary.

xfs_read_file reads regular files through inline extents or multi-level
bmap trees and reads local and remote symlink targets. It returns zeros for
holes and unwritten extents, handles partial blocks and 64-bit offsets, and clamps reads
at EOF. Inode/file APIs require a caller-owned block-sized scratch buffer
(up to 64 KiB, with at least 2 KiB for remote symlinks on 1 KiB-block filesystems);
their core routines allocate no memory. Keep the returned
inode image immutable with its original reader and disjoint from scratch.
Inode output is unchanged on error; a failed file read reports zero completed
bytes but may have altered its destination. Remote symlink buffers are checked
for checksum, owner, UUID, disk address, chunk length and target offset;
partial reads still verify the entire target. Targets are limited to 1023 bytes.

xfs_read_directory enumerates shortform, block, leaf and node directories.
It verifies the data entries, names, file types, inode-number bounds, dots,
free-space records, v3 identity/CRC, and a block directory's embedded hash
index. Directory blocks up to 64 KiB can span multiple filesystem blocks.
xfs_directory_lookup scans all data entries for an exact name, rejecting
duplicate matches. External leaf/node hash indexes and free-space indexes
are not consulted or verified; this uses the upstream data-only readdir
approach. ASCII case-insensitive directories are unsupported. Scans are
linear in directory data size and use no cache or readahead.

xfs_lookup_path resolves absolute image paths, repeated separators, dots,
relative/absolute symlinks and trailing slashes. It checks selected inode
allocation, limits expanded paths to 4095 bytes and traversal to 40 symlinks.
The caller can decline to follow the final symlink. Paths remain entirely
inside the image. There are no host pathname operations or permission checks.

Directory/path APIs require directory-block-size + filesystem-block-size
scratch, from xfs_directory_scratch_size. Path resolution also takes a
caller-owned xfs_path_workspace; use heap allocation in the server. Directory
callbacks receive byte names valid only during that call. Earlier callbacks
can have run when a later block fails. Lookup outputs are unchanged on error.

xfs_log_inspect scans the internal v2 journal's cycle stamps and checks the
latest record's boundaries, UUID, LSNs, sector/stripe alignment and CRC32C.
It supports extended headers and records wrapping across the physical end.
Only a complete, single unmount operation at the current head establishes
CLEAN; a checksummed record without that operation reports NEEDS_RECOVERY.
Malformed, ambiguous and unsupported logs return errors. The inspector
does not trim partial writes, fall back to an older unmount, clear stale
blocks or replay transactions. It accepts the special zero-CRC mkfs initial
unmount only with its canonical fields, padding and wholly zero suffix.

xfs_require_clean_log returns zero only for CLEAN. Call it before opening
inodes for filesystem service; xfs_reader_open alone remains a raw metadata
inspection API. The CLI now gates inode, file and pathname operations with
this check. Its --raw option explicitly bypasses the guard for image analysis;
the mount_xfs server provides no such bypass. A successful check
does not establish global consistency or validate metadata LSNs.

Journal scratch is XFS_LOG_SCRATCH_SIZE (260 KiB), caller-owned and reusable.
The scan reads the whole log in 64 KiB chunks, then reads the selected record;
it is linear in log size, with upstream limits on log and record lengths.
V1 logs, cycle-number rollover, nonmonotonic cycle patterns and noninitial
zero-CRC records are unsupported. Some logs recoverable by Linux will be
rejected. CRC verification covers the upstream 324/328-byte header layouts
and the used portions of extended headers, before restoring packed cycle data.

The probe recognizes v4 superblocks with v2 directories and unwritten extent
support, and v5 superblocks with the pinned upstream's feature definitions.
It rejects unknown incompatible bits, NEEDSREPAIR, nonzero log-incompatible
bits, incomplete mkfs state, external logs and realtime devices. Unknown
compatible and read-only-compatible bits do not prevent superblock inspection.
Inode reads reject unknown read-only-compatible features. Recognizing a
feature bit does not mean that all operations for that feature are ported.

This is an experimental mountable subset, not a complete filesystem verifier.
It does not inspect external directory indexes or external attribute contents,
cross-check global tree counts or allocation ownership, or validate metadata LSNs
against the log. Tree verification covers the visited paths;
it does not check unvisited subtrees or reciprocal sibling links.
Even a read-only mount can require recovery; a successful probe must never
be used as evidence that replay can be skipped.
There is no write or recovery path.
The command-line probe accepts regular image files only and opens them
read-only. The ordinary host tests never mount a filesystem; the separate
native PUFFS integration suite mounts disposable regular images on NetBSD.
Neither suite accesses a raw disk device.
Images must be quiescent for the entire inspection: this reader has no cache,
locking, snapshot, or concurrent-growfs semantics. An AGF ring that upstream
would reset is rejected rather than changed.

Layout
------

    dist/       Unmodified upstream files, retained for comparison.
    include/    Compatibility definitions and the probe interface.
    netbsd/     Selected upstream routines and NetBSD probe wrapper.
    puffs/      Native NetBSD mount_xfs server, explicit Makefile and manual.
    tests/      Host tests, image inspector, header checks and native mount tests.

Build and test from the src root
-------------------------------

    sh external/gpl2/xfs/tests/run.sh
    sh external/gpl2/xfs/tests/run.sh --images
    sh external/gpl2/xfs/tests/check-netbsd.sh

The first command needs a C compiler and runs 1,070 superblock checks, including
v4 quota translation, UUID selection, unaligned and short input, sector corruption,
invalid geometry and feature masks, and shortened final allocation groups.
It also runs 1,600 AG validation cases and I/O boundary/failure checks: each
header read is subjected to errors, short reads and incorrect byte counts;
CRC-protected bytes, recomputed-checksum corruptions, UUIDs, optional trees,
wrapped/full/empty free lists, legacy v4 slots, unaligned scratch buffers,
and output preservation are covered. CC and CFLAGS can override the compiler
and host-test flags.

The inode suite adds 8,879 checks covering v4/v5, sparse inode chunks,
multi-level inode and bmap trees, non-power-of-two AG encoding, the shortened
last AG, holes/unwritten data, local symlinks, empty files, offsets beyond
4 GiB, EOF, unaligned buffers, checksum and semantic corruption, cycles,
callback errors/short reads, and output preservation. It also checks ownership,
legacy/v3 link counts, allocation overflow, legacy/bigtime timestamps and
block-aligned inode chunks that do not start at multiples of 64.
Fragmented bmaps and
sparse/unwritten layouts currently use synthetic fixtures.

The namespace suite adds 8,350 checks for v4/v5 shortform and block/data
directories, 32/64-bit inode numbers, free entries, hash collisions, callbacks,
holes, lookup output preservation, corruption with recomputed CRCs, and remote
symlinks. Contiguous and fragmented two-block v5 symlinks with 1 KiB blocks
are covered by synthetic fixtures, including corruption in an unrequested
tail and insufficient scratch capacity.

The journal suite adds 1,009 checks plus guard/argument checks for v4/v5
superblocks, both endian formats, extended headers, wrapped records, skipped
magic cycle numbers, record CRCs, canonical initialization, I/O failures,
unaligned scratch, output preservation, torn writes and stale unmounts.

The attribute suite adds 1,272 checks for v4/v5 inline forks, empty retained
forks, all flag combinations, truncation, duplicate names, binary/empty values,
255-byte names/values, 255-entry lists in 2 KiB inodes, callback failures,
and rejection before callbacks when a later entry is malformed. It also tests
the root-property allowlist, inode identity/type, mixed allowed/denied
attributes, and explicit rejection of ACLs, security labels, parent pointers
and external forks.

The second additionally requires Python 3.11 or newer and xfsprogs (mkfs.xfs
and xfs_db, plus xfs_logprint for the journal tests). It creates six disposable
400 MiB sparse images, compares the
superblock, all 24 AGs and 30 populated inodes with xfs_db, checks regular-file
and local/remote-symlink contents against mkfs prototype sources.
Whole-image hashes before and after inspection confirm
unchanged contents. It also tests seven bad superblocks, twelve bad AG headers
and five bad inodes, including corrupt metadata with recomputed checksums.
The configurations cover defaults, 4 KiB sectors, 64 KiB blocks, 2 KiB inodes,
a shortened final AG and disabled optional trees. Tests were run with
xfsprogs 7.1.1. A future mkfs default may enable a newer, unsupported feature;
review the upstream baseline rather than bypassing the rejection.

Four additional populated images test directory listings against xfs_db and
file reads by path, using default, 8 KiB-directory, 64 KiB-directory and
1 KiB-filesystem-block configurations. The default fixture explicitly checks
shortform, block, leaf and node formats, with up to 1800 files per directory.
Tests include deep paths, relative/absolute links, loops, dangling links,
trailing slashes, missing names, non-directories, long components, expansion
limits and 24 directory/symlink corruption cases. Image hashes also cover
restoration of the intentionally mutated disposable blocks.

xfsprogs 7.1.1's prototype writer produced a truncated remote symlink in the
1 KiB-block test: a 990-byte inode size with only one 968-byte payload mapping.
The reader correctly rejects that image. The mkfs test uses a shorter target
for this configuration; synthetic fixtures test valid two-block targets.

Six additional images compare journal head/tail and cleanliness with
xfs_logprint, including v4, 4 KiB sectors and 64/256 KiB log stripe units.
They test guarded root lookup, dirty/clean transitions, corrupt/torn records,
explicit raw inspection and a synthetic wrapped record in a mkfs image.
No journal fixture was captured from a running Linux mount yet; ordinary
noninitial records and crash cases currently use synthetic data.

The third compiles all fourteen port files, the CLI, PUFFS server and native
test client with this checkout's
NetBSD amd64 user-space headers and no host headers. It needs an amd64
compiler. This verifies header/layout compatibility, not linking or running
on NetBSD. Native testing is described below.

An undefined-behavior check that does not require sanitizer runtime libraries:

    CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=undefined -fsanitize-undefined-trap-on-error' \
        sh external/gpl2/xfs/tests/run.sh

To build a persistent image inspector on NetBSD or Linux:

    xfs_src=external/gpl2/xfs
    cc -std=gnu11 -D_DEFAULT_SOURCE -O2 -I "$xfs_src/include" \
        -isystem "$xfs_src/dist" "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_sb.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_probe.c" "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_cksum.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_io.c" "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_ag.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_read_log.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_read_btree.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_read_inode.c" "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_read_file.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_inode_stat.c" "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_read_attr.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_read_symlink.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_read_directory.c" "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_lookup_path.c" \
        "$xfs_src/tests/xfs_probe.c" \
        -o /tmp/xfs_probe
    /tmp/xfs_probe /path/to/image
    /tmp/xfs_probe -j /path/to/image
    /tmp/xfs_probe -a 0 /path/to/image
    /tmp/xfs_probe -i 131 /path/to/image
    /tmp/xfs_probe -r 131 0 4096 /path/to/image > /tmp/file-prefix
    /tmp/xfs_probe -l / /path/to/image
    /tmp/xfs_probe -p /directory/file /path/to/image
    /tmp/xfs_probe -R /directory/file 0 4096 /path/to/image > /tmp/file-prefix
    /tmp/xfs_probe -s /directory/symlink /path/to/image

Paths and inode numbers in these examples are illustrative. -l emits JSON
lines with ino, type (XFS on-disk type) and name; non-ASCII/control name bytes
are escaped as \u00xx. -p inspects metadata and -R reads bytes after following
symlinks; -s returns the final symlink's target. -r, -R and -s emit raw bytes
without a trailing newline. Discard partial output if a command fails.
-j prints journal state, head, tail, latest record, head cycle and whether
the canonical initial record was used. These addresses are log-relative
512-byte block numbers. An inspection can report needs-recovery successfully;
guarded file/path commands reject that state. Use --raw before the other CLI
options only when deliberately inspecting potentially unrecovered metadata.

Native PUFFS build and test
--------------------------

On NetBSD with the compiler set and libpuffs installed:

    make -C external/gpl2/xfs/puffs
    mkdir /tmp/xfs-mount
    external/gpl2/xfs/puffs/mount_xfs -s -o ro /path/to/image /tmp/xfs-mount

Run the mount command as root; -s stays in the foreground. In another shell,
inspect the mounted files, then unmount with "umount /tmp/xfs-mount".
Omit -s to detach. No helper is installed automatically.

Create fixtures on a host with Python 3.11+ and xfsprogs (tested with 7.1.1):

    PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 external/gpl2/xfs/tests/make_puffs_images.py /tmp/xfs-mount-fixtures

The destination must be new. This creates v4/v5 images with the default
autofsck property, readable user attributes, and rejected ACL/security,
unknown trusted, non-root property and external-block examples. Other inodes
have malformed shortform attributes with valid CRCs. Four startup rejection
images cover root ACLs, unknown trusted attributes, malformed attributes and
a bad journal. Only these disposable fixtures have inherited prototype
SELinux labels removed; selected files receive explicit rejection fixtures.
The root property is temporarily removed to add a synthetic hard link, then
restored through xfs_db. Hard links, fixed legacy/bigtime timestamps, binary
attribute bytes and corruptions are synthetic on-disk additions with checksums
regenerated. Transfer the fixtures sparsely if testing in a VM.
Then, as root on NetBSD:

    sh external/gpl2/xfs/tests/run-puffs.sh /tmp/xfs-mount-fixtures

The script builds the real server and client, mounts both versions, and tests
file/partial/EOF reads, local/remote symlinks, timestamps, owner/group/other
permissions, directory search, inherited descriptors, hard-link lock identity,
1800-entry listings, telldir/seekdir and small-buffer getdents resumption.
It checks native attribute sizes/values, empty and binary data, both list
formats, partial buffers, descriptor operations, permission checks, and
attribute/ACL rejection on lookup and at startup.
It checks read-only errors, busy/ordinary/signal unmounts, persistent ESTALE
after a deliberate image timestamp change, startup refusal and unchanged
whole-image SHA256 hashes. Use disposable fixtures only: their mtime changes.
The native build and mount suite were validated on NetBSD 11.0/amd64 in QEMU,
using the release's kernel, headers and libpuffs. The compile-only check also
covers this checkout's headers; other architectures remain untested.

Remaining milestones
--------------------

1. Extend attribute reading to external leaf/node blocks and remote values.
   Interpret ACL/security semantics explicitly,
   so ordinary labelled images can be served without weakening permissions.
   Expand runtime coverage for abrupt server death, parallel clients and
   node reclamation under memory pressure. Add bounded caching only with
   explicit consistency and lifetime contracts.
2. Add Linux-produced clean/dirty journals, fragmented, sparse and unwritten
   files, complementing synthetic fixtures. Port external directory index
   verifiers/hash lookup and expand cross-tree and metadata-LSN validation.
   Port transaction reservations, logging/recovery and allocation in user
   space before enabling writes. Require crash/replay tests and xfstests
   coverage, including concurrency and forced-shutdown paths.

Useful references
-----------------

    https://wiki.netbsd.org/projects/project/xfs/
    https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/xfs/index.html
    https://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/utils/fs/xfs/docs/xfs_filesystem_structure.pdf

NetBSD's lib/libpuffs/puffs.h, puffs_ops.3, puffs_node.3 and puffs_cred.3,
share/examples/puffs, and usr.sbin/puffs supply the local integration contracts
and examples. The server uses libpuffs directly; the XFS engine stays in the
user-space process. GPL provenance and the pinned upstream baseline remain
unchanged. XFS transaction, locking and cache contracts still require explicit
adaptation; the scalar compatibility header is not a substitute for that work.
